ia64: close the instruction group after strncmp's alloc

The rewritten ia64 strncmp() returns straight out of the entry group when
n == 0:

	alloc	r2 = ar.pfs, 3, 0, 0, 0
	adds	tmp = 1, sx1
	mov	one = 1
	mov	ret0 = r0
	cmp.eq	p7, p0 = n, r0
  (p7)	br.ret.spnt.many b0
	;;

There is no stop bit between the alloc and the br.ret, so both land in one
instruction group.  alloc writes CFM and the RSE, br.ret writes CFM from
ar.pfs and reads the RSE, which is a dependency violation - "as -xexplicit"
reports all three:

  Warning: Use of 'br.ret.spnt.many' may violate WAW dependency 'CFM'
  Warning: Use of 'br.ret.spnt.many' may violate RAW dependency 'RSE'
  Warning: Use of 'br.ret.spnt.many' may violate WAW dependency 'RSE'

The register frame the caller gets back is then undefined.  In practice the
caller's frame is truncated to strncmp's own three registers, so its high
locals - where gcc likes to park the saved gp - read back as garbage.  A
caller that restores gp with "mov r1=rNN" after the call then continues with
a bogus gp and dies on the next gp relative access or PLT call; a caller that
touches a high local first takes an Illegal Operation fault instead, because
the register is now outside the frame.

This bites everything that calls strncmp with n == 0 from a function with a
large frame.  GNU make dies parsing "$(patsubst %/,%,...)", where the suffix
compare is strneq(..., pattern_postpercent_len - 1) with a one character
suffix; gcc dies with SIGILL the same way.  The old loop based strncmp exited
through a separate label and never hit this.

strncmp is the only place in libc.so where an alloc shares its instruction
group with a CFM writing branch - compiler generated code never does it.

Signed-off-by: René Rebe <rene@exactco.de>

--- glibc-2.44/sysdeps/ia64/strncmp.S
+++ glibc-2.44/sysdeps/ia64/strncmp.S
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
    This file is part of the GNU C Library.
    Copyright (C) 2000-2024 Free Software Foundation, Inc.
    Copyright (C) 2024-2026 EPIC Linux project
+   Copyright (C) 2026 René Rebe <rene@exactco.de>
 
    The GNU C Library is free software; you can redistribute it and/or
    modify it under the terms of the GNU Lesser General Public
@@ -48,6 +49,7 @@
 
 ENTRY(strncmp)
 	alloc	r2 = ar.pfs, 3, 0, 0, 0
+	;;				// alloc must not share its instruction group with the br.ret below
 	adds	tmp = 1, sx1		// pre-compute sx1 + 1 for both sentinel (sx1 + n + 1) and sy1 (sx1 + 1)
 	mov	one = 1
 	mov	ret0 = r0
